{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli after his death in 1152. He was a minor at the time so his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem and his wife Morphia of Melitene. Raymond III had a younger brother Bohemond who died in infancy. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli after his death in 1152.\n\nRaymond III's early life is not well documented but it is known that he was educated in the court of his uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. He was a minor at the time of his succession so his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. Hodierna played an important role in the early years of Raymond III's reign and helped to secure his position as Count of Tripoli.\n\n*The Crusader States and the Muslim World*\nRaymond III played an important role in the Crusader States during his reign. He was a member of the Haute Cour of Jerusalem and played a key role in the decision-making process of the kingdom. Raymond III also had to deal with the threat of the Muslim world during his reign. He fought against the forces of Nur ad-Din Zangi and later Saladin.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and the Fall of Jerusalem*\nRaymond III played a key role in the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the leaders of the Crusader army and fought alongside King Guy of Jerusalem. The battle was a disaster for the Crusaders and many of them were killed or captured. Raymond III was one of the few leaders who escaped the battle. He later joined the Siege of Acre but died in 1187 before the city was captured.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logpr